  "strategy": "**Approach and Rationale**\nThis approach uses a **complementarity representation** where the function h(x) is defined as the complement of another function f(x) that must satisfy certain properties. The key insight is that by modeling h(x) as part of a physical system with potential energy between particles, we can derive a constrained optimization problem that is easier to solve.\n\nWe define:\n- h(x) = min(1, max(0, a(x))) where a(x) is an adjusted function\n- The complement function f(x) = 1 - h(x)\n- The optimization objective is designed to be convex in a novel embedding\n\nThe central innovation is the **relative entropy constraint** that regularizes the solution space, reducing dependence on lower-dimensional gradient methods. This allows us to:\n\n1. Break the problem into smaller, more tractable stages\n2. Use warm-start techniques from previous optimal solutions\n3. Incorporate problem-specific smoothing objectives\n\nThis differs from the parent approach by:\n- Using constraint-based embedding\n- Introducing novel problem-specific regularization\n- Leverages multiple time-scale optimization\n\n**Mathematical Specification**\n- Variables: a(x) \u2208 [-\u221e,1] \u222a [1,\u221e) for all x\n- Objective: Minimize \u2225\u2207(h(x))\u2225\u00b2 subject to \u222bh(x)dx=1\n- Representation: Piecewise solutions on sparse grid\n- Constraints: \n  1. \u222bh(x)dx=1\n  2. h(x) \u2208 [0,1] everywhere\n  3. h(x) has unit L2-variation\n\nThe optimization proceeds in two stages:\n1. Find candidate functions a(x) that satisfy complementary constraints\n2. Project back to h(x) using entropic mapping\n\n**Implementation Procedure**\n1. **Runtime Setup**:\n   - Check for parent solution if available\n   - Initialize sparse grid (n=64 initially)\n   - Compute parity using problem-specific compensated features\n\n2. **Core Stages**:\n   - **Stage 1: Candidate Generation** (low resolution)\n     ```\n     For candidate grid points g_i uniformly sampled from initial set:\n       Define h_i(x) = min(1, max(0, a_i(x))) \n       where a_i(x) solves proximity equations to target\n       Compute C5 on h_i using full cross-correlation\n     ```\n   - **Stage 2: Projection & Embedding** (progressive resolution)\n     ```\n     Use candidate pool from Stage1 to warm-start high-res solver\n     Implement relative-entropy constraints with automatic differentiation\n     Apply multi-scale post-processing to ensure [0,1] bounds\n     ```\n\n3. **Search Choices**:\n   - Auto-tuned step sizes based on gradient descent history\n   - Dynamic grid selection: 64\u2192128\u2192256\u2192512\u21921024\n   - Co-scheduling of parent solution parallel trials\n\n4. **Compute Budget**:\n   - Capped Stage1 at n=64 runs ($100sfu budget)\n   - Stage2 proportions: 60% for projection, 30% for high-res cleanup\n   - Check points every 500s for early termination\n\n5. **Validation**:\n   - Verify integral constraint: \u2211h[i] * dx = 1 (within 1e-5)\n   - Ensure [0,1] bounds: max(h[i])\u22641, min(h[i])\u22650\n   - Recompute C5 on resolution-matched grid\n   - Maintain parent fallback at resolution boundaries\n\n**Search Choices and Variations**\n- Use relative-entropy initialization weights \u03b1 based on parent entropy\n- Employ Jacobian-free Newton-Krylov acceleration (if budget>1500s)\n- Multiple restart patterns: symmetric, antisymmetric, random\n- Progressive resolution doubling with cosine scheduling\n\n**Compute Budget and Robustness**\n- Dominant cost: Stage2 evaluation at 1024 points\n- Memory profile: 128-byte per candidate vector\n- Fall-back: Return least-inferior parent solution if search stalls\n- Budget allocation:\n  - 10% Stage1 candidate generation\n  - 40% Projection from low-res to high-res\n  - 40% High-resolution cleanup and verification\n  - 10% Automatic budget re-allocation\n\n**Validation and Return Contract**\n- Check final solution against all constraints\n- Compute exact C5 on target resolution\n- Ensure objective is non-decreasing during optimization\n- Maintain separation between development and deployment costs\n- If budget threshold exceeded, return best-known parent",
